<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Erhai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Erhai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Erhai is a large highland lake in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, lying beneath the Cang mountains. The plain beside it was the centre of the six chiefdoms united as Nanzhao in the 8th century and of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a>, whose capitals, Taihe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangjumie">Yangjumie</a>, stood near its western shore. It remains the heart of the Bai people's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> around the city of Dali.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-erhai.jpg" alt="Lake Erhai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ariel Steiner (Asteiner) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erhai_lake,_Yunnan,_China.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
